    89	He sat down on the pump trough with his forearms on his knees. There were two red bars across them.
    90	
    91	"Three days," said Lira. She had hitched herself up onto a capstan bar and was swinging her feet. "And then three people in a draughty hall write *Iron* on you, and you're a rank nobody can take off."
    92	
    93	"They'll find it."
    94	
    95	"You say that as if it were bad weather."
    96	
    97	"I say it like I say anything." Brom turned his forearms over and looked at the marks. "I'll stand in it, and I'll hold what they set, and the plate will say what's so. I want that. I've wanted it since before I had a Path." He was quiet a moment. "It's the paper after that I've stopped being able to see properly. Somebody hands you a sheet. It says *found*, and there's a line at the bottom for your name."
    98	
    99	Lira stopped swinging her feet.
   100	
   101	"I went and looked at the district hall yesterday," she said. "From outside. It's three streets over. It's got a blue door, and a board beside it with the sittings on, and the first one after the year's close has six names. Mine's the fourth. Somebody's written *Wind* after it, and then *Halcenvane*." She picked at the tape on her wrist. "I stood there a good while. A carter asked me if I was lost."
   102	
   103	"Were you?"
   104	
   105	"Ask me after the Fifth-day."
   106	
   107	Neither of them said anything more. Brom pumped water over his forearms, and Lira jumped down and went to look at the screw's thread, and after a while she asked him how you would grease a thing like that, and he told her.
   108	
   109	On the gallery Cael stood with his hand on the satchel's new strap.
   110	
   111	They had each finished something, and he could see it from up here as he could see a floor from a tier. Brom's left shoulder took its whole share. Lira landed where she chose. Whatever a panel or a blue door might add to either of them was a line on a sheet. They had done the work in a harness room and a painted hall and a wool yard, with nobody writing.
   112	
   113	And there were two boxes roped under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat.
   114	
   115	He had told himself all year that whatever this cost him, it should not cost them. Karis had read that over his shoulder and said it was not how any of them were built. Looking down at the two of them at the pump, he understood for the first time what she had meant by it. It was not his to decide what they paid. It never had been. He could only see to it that when the day came, each of them was asked, and not carried.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	Karis filed her last paper that afternoon, and he went with her to carry the wallet.
   120	
   121	It was three leaves. She had written it at the parlour's long table in the morning, in pencil first, from habit, and then in ink, and the counsel had read it standing and signed the foot without altering a word.
   122	
   123	"It's upon the entry they read out," said Karis, as they crossed the lane. "The certificate. The code lets a seat go on by its own authority where the certificate's withheld; I looked at the section the night we got home. So I went back through the digests to see how often a seat ever had." She held up four fingers. "Four times. I've set them out, with their years. A guild hall's frame, where the assessor wouldn't sign. Two station frames in the hill districts. A plate. Four times in the whole record a seat has proceeded without the certifying office's name, and every time it was upon a thing. Never once upon a person."
   124	
   125	"It won't—"
   126	
   127	"I know it won't. Don't say it; you said it on the floor, and I only needed it once." She was not cross. "It's inert. It goes in under my name and it sits there. But the count's true, and nobody else was going to make it, and I didn't like the seat to rule without having been told."
   128	
   129	The seat's office was a hatch in the exchange's side passage, with a brass grille and a shelf. The junior chair sat behind it. Cael had never seen the man so close. He was a man with a careful face and a clerk's stoop, and he took the three leaves from Karis without lifting his eyes above her hands. He counted them and looked at the signature and entered them in a book against the hour. He wrote a receipt in a plain square hand, and tore it at the rule, and slid it under the grille.
   130	
   131	"Received," he said. "It will be before the seat."
   132	
   133	"Thank you," said Karis.
   134	
   135	His right hand was opening and closing a little, under the shelf, as if it ached. He did not look up at either of them, and after a moment he drew the next thing in his tray toward him.
   136	
   137	In the lane Karis folded the receipt into the wallet's back pocket, with all the others.
   138	
   139	"That's the last one in this proceeding," she said, and shut the wallet. "I thought I'd feel more."
   140	
   141	---
   142	
   143	On the third day he walked north.
   144	
   145	He was within his rights. The counsel had drawn the bound for him on a scrap of paper at breakfast: the bridge, the wharf, the east gate, and on the north road the toll-bar, beyond which a man could no longer be said to hear the bell. Inside that line he might go where he liked. He said where he was going to Brom, who nodded, and to Bracken, who wrote it down.
   146	
   147	The coat came with him. It was the one from the upper window, the older of the pair, a grey patient man with a walking stick he did not need. He followed at forty yards, in plain sight, down the middle of the street, as Seln had taught four people in an orchard to walk when they wished to be written down as what they were. Cael found that he did not mind it. They had been walking the same roads for a long time.
   148	
   149	The north road left the town between tanyards and came out among garden plots and low walls. A little short of the toll-bar, on the left, stood the district station.
   150	
   151	He had known it was there. He had not come to see it. He saw it, and stopped at the horse trough on the far side of the road, and did not go on.
   152	
   153	It was a low stone house with a slate roof, and behind it a second building, windowless, with a wide door, which was the frame-house. Over the front door a sigil was cut in the lintel. There was a bench under the wall, in the sun, and a board beside the door with the day's hours chalked on it in a round hand. It was the most ordinary building he had ever seen. There was one like it at the edge of every town on the continent that had a registry, and he had not stood in front of one since Denvash.
   154	
   155	A girl sat on the bench with her mother.
   156	
   157	She was about fourteen. She had on a clean brown dress that had been let down at the hem, and good boots, and she sat with her hands in her lap and her knees together and her back very straight, as she had plainly been told to sit. And somebody had done her hair. It was plaited in two braids and the braids were pinned up round her head in a crown, tight and even, with not a strand loose, and a narrow green ribbon was worked through the plait from one side to the other. It was not a style for an ordinary day. It had taken somebody the best part of an hour, early, by a window, with a mouth full of pins.
   158	
   159	The mother sat beside her with a basket on her knees. Every little while her hand came up, without her seeming to know it, and touched the braid above the girl's ear, to be sure of a pin.
   160	
   161	Cael stood at the trough.
   162	
   163	He knew what was in that building. He knew it as he knew the inside of Hesk's shop. There was a room with a brass plate in it. The girl would be told to stand still, and to put her hand where she was shown, and something in her that had been asleep for fourteen years would wake, and the plate would light. In the frame-house a keeper would read what the frame gave him, and write it in his station book in two figures, a reading and a margin.
   164	
   165	And then the thing that was appended to the reading, and was not the reading, would advise what was to be entered. It would be entered as advised.
   166	
   167	He did not know that it would be different from what the plate had shown. Most were not. There had been forty pairs in a locked case, from a few districts over a few years, and Karis would not guess past them and Seln had stopped estimating. This girl might walk out of that door with the truth on a slip of paper. He would never know. That was the whole of the horror of it, standing in the sun by a trough. Neither would she. If the margin had run past her column, nobody would enter it, and somebody would be told to observe her for nine weeks, and would see a girl with a green ribbon going about her life, and would write *nothing to report*.
   168	
   169	The door opened.
   170	
   171	The district officer who came out was a stout, fair, unhurried man in registry grey with his sleeves turned back, and he had a board in his hand. He looked at the bench and smiled. It was a real smile. He said a name that Cael was too far off to catch, and then something else, and the girl laughed in spite of herself and stood up.
   172	
   173	The mother stood too, and was told, kindly, by a hand held out flat, that she was to wait. She sat down again. The girl went in under the sigil with her crown of braids, and the officer held the door for her as he might have held it for his own daughter.
   174	
   175	The new thing in Cael lifted its head. It always did, at a threshold, and this was more of a threshold than most. *There?*
   176	
   177	*Door*, he told it. *A station's. It's theirs. Leave it.*
   178	
   179	It left it.
   180	
   181	He waited. He did not count, for once. The mother sat with the basket and looked at the door. The coat, forty yards back, had stopped by a garden wall and was looking at a row of beans. He had seen a thousand stations, and this one had nothing in it for his column.
   182	
   183	It did not take long. It had taken eleven seconds once, in another town, and a woman behind a desk had said a word, and he had walked out into a different life. This took perhaps as long as it takes to boil an egg.
   184	
   185	The girl came out with a slip of paper in her hand.
   186	
   187	She was reading it as she came, and she had gone pink to the ears. She said something to her mother. Cael saw her lips make the word, and then, because once was not enough, make it again. The mother put the basket down on the bench and took the girl's face in both hands, carefully, with her thumbs clear of the braids, and kissed her on the forehead.
   188	
   189	In the doorway the officer stood and watched them. He was pleased. Anybody could have seen it. He had done a thing he had done ten thousand times, and done it properly, and a child was happy, and he liked his work. He looked at his board, and turned it over, and went in to see who was next.
   190	
   191	Cael would have staked the notebook on that man. He would have staked Hesk's book. There was not a lie anywhere in him.
   192	
   193	They went away toward the town, the two of them, the girl holding the slip and reading it over as she walked. They passed within six feet of him. She had a scatter of freckles and one front tooth a little crooked, and she did not see him at all.
   194	
   195	He stood at the trough until they were out of sight.
   196	
   197	He had been arguing about himself for a year: his enrollment and his file, the nine days of his renewal, his frame. And the whole while, on every north road in the Compact, on every morning the stations opened, this had been going on.
   198	
   199	The seat would rule in two days. He had thought of the ruling, all year, as the thing that would decide. It would decide what the registry did. He understood, with the sun on the back of his neck, that it would leave him with a decision of his own all the same, and that it was not the one he had been preparing for. It had nothing to do with whether he could win. It was whether he would stand in a frame, knowing what was appended to it, and let it enter him as advised.
   200	
   201	He walked back. The coat fell in forty yards behind.
   202	
   203	That night, at the window, with the cupola dark against the sky and the bell quiet under it, he opened Hesk's book and wrote the day's entry.
   204	
   205	*Third-day. The district station, the north road. A girl of fourteen. Her mother had done her hair.*
   206	
   207	He looked at it, and then blotted it and shut the book. He had not written a shorter entry all term, and he did not add to it.
   208	
   209	---
   210	
   211	The call came on the fourth morning, at the second bell.
   212	
   213	It came by the seat's own messenger, a boy in grey with a strap of papers, who would not give his leaf to the woman of the house and stood in the passage until Bracken came down. Bracken read it on the stair with his first pair of glasses and then with his second.
   214	
   215	"The evaluation seat will make its return," he said. "In the long room, at the third bell. The parties are called." He lowered the leaf. "It is a return, and not a sitting. I should like that understood by everybody before we cross the lane. I said fourteen. I am holding to fourteen."
   216	
   217	"What does an evaluation seat return?" said Lira.
   218	
   219	"Whatever it found within its scope and is obliged to lay before the seat. Generally nothing. Generally one line, to say there is nothing." Bracken folded the leaf in three. "She has asked for the room, and for the clerk, and for the records table. That is a great deal of furniture for one line."
   220	
   221	The long room was empty.
   222	
   223	Cael had not known how large it was. He had seen it with thirty people in it and with two hundred, and never as it stood now, with the forty benches running back bare to the doors and the lot numbers showing in the floor between them. The high east windows laid four long bars of light across the boards. There was nobody on the steps outside, for nobody had been told. A return was posted in the porch after it was made.
   224	
   225	Eleven people. He counted them, because it took no time. Jent and his thin clerk. Withrow, the counsel, Karis, Bracken and himself. The seat's clerk. The two at the records table.
   226	
   227	On the platform the presiding chair stood behind its long table with a grey cover drawn over it.
   228	
   229	He looked at that for longer than he looked at anything else. For thirteen sittings there had been a hood in that place, and he had tried each time to learn something from how it sat. There was a cloth there now, hanging in folds, as there had been cloths on the five chairs below it before the third sitting. It told him exactly as much.
   230	
   231	Four of the five chairs were empty too. Ilsev had come alone.
   232	
   233	She stood up from the middle one. She was a spare woman of sixty or so with her grey hair cut short, in a plain coat, and she had her file open on her left forearm at the angle at which he had seen it for a year. She did not come forward. She stood where she had sat, and looked at the seat's clerk.
   234	
   235	"The evaluation seat makes its return," she said. "I shall read it at the pace of dictation. I ask that it be taken down as read, and not from my leaf afterward."
   236	
   237	"As read," said the clerk, and dipped his pen. At the records table the junior chair wrote the time.
   238	
   239	Beside Cael, Karis had opened the wallet. She had no table for ink in a wool yard, and had not needed one for a week. She took out the square of planed deal and a pencil, and set the pencil's point on a clean sheet.
   240	
   241	"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
   242	
   243	She turned a leaf in her file.
   244	
   245	"This officer has twice referred that designation, in the proper form, to learn the office that entered it. She has received two returns. She will read both into the record, entire, as they came to her."
   246	
   247	Nothing in the empty room moved.
   248	
   249	"The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
   250	
   251	Karis's pencil did not stop. Cael watched it go across the deal in her small upright hand, a word behind the voice.
   252	
   253	"The second. *Referral closed at registry level; no further inquiry authorized.*"
   254	
   255	Ilsev turned the leaf back.
   256	
   257	"And the note which this officer has written beside that designation upon every form she has filed concerning the subject, and which she now enters here. *Authorizing office: not traced.*"
   258	
   259	She lifted her eyes from the file. She did not look at the house's table, or at Jent, but at the grey cover on the presiding chair.
   260	
   261	"The evaluation seat notes the contradiction. The registry relies upon a designation to govern an evaluation, and the registry's own returns say that no office of record entered it. The evaluation seat makes no finding upon that. It is not within the scope minuted for this seat. This officer has been told that no further inquiry is authorized, and she makes none. She reads what she was sent." A last pause, no longer than the others. "That is the return."
   262	
   263	She sat down, and set the file at its angle.
   264	
   265	Cael looked up at the clock over the doors. Six minutes.
   266	
   267	He sat with his hands flat on the wood, and they were quite steady, and he was glad of that.
   268	
   269	*Designation predates file creation.*
   270	
   271	He had been fourteen. A room at Denvash, a desk, eleven seconds. Karis had laid it out for him on a night of frost, with her gloves on: something that was not the reading had looked for a column to put him in, and had found none, and had done the safe thing. It had written a word where an older name had been. And it had set a watch on him, at that same instant, before a clerk had so much as ruled a sheet with his name at the top.
   272	
   273	There had been no file yet. The registry had just said so. It had said so twice, under seal, in a clerk's round hand, and a careful woman with short grey hair had read it out at the pace of dictation in an empty wool exchange so that it would be taken down right.
   274	
   275	She did not know what it meant. He was as sure of that as he had been of the man at the station door. She had found a hole in the record and been told not to look into it, and she had done the one thing that was left inside her clearance, which was to say aloud, in public, that it was there.
   276	
   277	"Received from the evaluation seat," said the clerk, "and entered in the minute. A fair copy will stand in the public record before the ruling. Has either party an observation?"
   278	
   279	Jent rose. He looked, for the first time in a year, a little tired.
   280	
   281	"The petitioner set that designation out in his schedule because it is upon the file," he said. "He was not told its office either. He has no observation."
   282	
   283	"The respondent?"
   284	
   285	The counsel stood. "None," she said, and sat.
   286	
   287	That was all. The clerk shut his book.
   288	
   289	On the steps, in the empty street, Karis stopped with the deal board against her chest. She had the whole of it, in pencil, in seventeen lines, and she was looking at the eleventh.
   290	
   291	"That's the truest sentence anybody has entered about you in a year of sittings," she said, "and the woman who read it out has no idea what it says."
   292	
   293	She put the board into the wallet, and did up the strap.

