     1	# Chapter 51 — The Hand
     2	
     3	Havel, in the junior chair at the records table, had written the time when the senior evaluator stood, and the time when she sat, and the difference between them was six minutes.
     4	
     5	He looked at the two figures after the room had emptied. He had written a great many pairs of times in that book since the fortieth day, and he could not remember looking at a pair before, for it was not his place to notice how long a thing had taken. It was his place to see that anybody who wanted to know could find out. He blotted them.
     6	
     7	"Junior," said the senior chair. He had the minute open in front of him and was running his thumb down the fresh page. "This wants to be in the porch before the ruling. That's the code, for a return. I've a tooth that won't let me climb, and the clerk's gone to his dinner. Take it up to the loft and stand over them while they copy it. Fair, one sheet, and you compare it yourself."
     8	
     9	"Yes," said Havel.
    10	
    11	He took the minute book in both hands. It was heavier than it looked, as a book is that has had a year written into it.
    12	
    13	The copyists' loft was over the long room, up a ladder-stair behind the platform. It had been the factors' sample loft, and there were still hooks in the beams where fleeces had hung to be judged by the light, though four slanted desks stood under the north windows now. In a sitting week the seat hired four copyists from the counting-houses along the wharf; on a call day it had kept only one, a young woman with a quick neat hand and a smudge of ink on her chin.
    14	
    15	He laid the book open on her desk, and showed her the place, and where it ended.
    16	
    17	"As it stands," he said. "Every stop."
    18	
    19	She began. He stood at her shoulder, as the manual said an officer was to stand when a minute left the records table, and read each line as she made it against the line it came from.
    20	
    21	He had not read it yet himself; he had only heard it, and he had always known that those were different things. He had sat forty feet from the middle chair with his pen moving, and had written a time, and had kept his eyes on his own page while a voice he had worked under for years said a thing slowly into an empty room. Now he read it.
    22	
    23	The second of the two returns he had by heart. He had read it once before, upside down, on a desk at Norhold, in perhaps three seconds, and had written it that night at the head of a sheet of his own, which lay in his kit at the inn, the fifth of six. He watched the copyist make the words, and they were the same words, and he felt nothing about them that he had not felt already.
    24	
    25	The first he had never seen. He had carried the file it answered; he had laid that file at her elbow in a hall on a bluff, square to the edge, and gone back to the foot of the table. A return had gone into it then, and he had not read it, because a records officer did not read what was addressed to the senior seat.
    26	
    27	*Determination: no originating authority of record.*
    28	
    29	The copyist's pen went across it without a check, and his eyes stayed on the line when her pen had gone on.
    30	
    31	His oldest sheet said that there was a mark in a boy's file with no office behind it. He had written that after he asked, in the proper way, and was told behind a shut door that it was not his clearance, and he had taken that to mean that somebody above him knew whose mark it was. In all these years he had wanted nothing from that mark but the name of an office. He would not have needed to be told the name. It would have been enough to know that one was written down somewhere, in a register he could not open, against a date.
    32	
    33	The registers had been asked properly, by a seat senior enough to ask, and they had answered. They knew of no office either.
    34	
    35	He stood very still at the copyist's shoulder. It was an answer, and he made himself be exact about that, as he was exact about a count. He had kept six sheets of things the registers could not answer, and here, in a minute, under a seal, was the registry's own hand saying of the first of them that it could not. He had not been wrong, on a pump step, when he was a younger man. He had only been alone with it.
    36	
    37	"Sir," said the copyist.
    38	
    39	She had stopped, with her pen lifted over the next line.
    40	
    41	"This can't be right. *Designation predates file creation.* A mark can't be older than the file it's written in. That's like a stamp being older than its letter." She looked up at him, quite earnestly, with the ink on her chin. "I think somebody's dropped a word. Shall I query it?"
    42	
    43	Havel looked at the line in the minute. It was in the senior chair's hand, taken down at dictation, and the senior chair did not drop words.
    44	
    45	"It is as it was read," he said.
    46	
    47	"But it doesn't make sense."
    48	
    49	"It is not required to. A fair copy says what the minute says. If the minute is wrong, that is for the seat, and it will be wrong in the copy too, so that the seat can find it." He heard his own voice, level and dry, saying what the manual said. "Copy it as it stands."
    50	
    51	She copied it as it stood. She was a good copyist, and she made the capital on *Designation* exactly the height of the one in the book.
    52	
    53	He did not let himself go on to the thing she had seen. He was aware of it, as a man is aware of a stair he has decided not to climb. A girl from a counting-house had read that sentence once and known at a glance that it could not be so; the senior evaluator had read it out in a wool exchange, and called it a contradiction, and made no finding. She had done what her clearance allowed, to its edge, and stopped there. He knew what it was to stop there. He had been doing it for years with a kit bag.
    54	
    55	He compared the copy, line by line, with a dry pen, and it agreed. He wrote *compared* at its foot, and the hour, and his name.
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	He pinned it in the porch himself.
    60	
    61	The exchange's porch had a board for the seat's public record, behind a brass grille that opened with the junior chair's key. He put the sheet up with four pins, and squared it by the grille's bars, and locked it.
    62	
    63	There was nobody on the steps. A boy came across from the trough, one of the lads the correspondents paid to watch the board, and read the heading with his lips moving: *Return of the evaluation seat.* He looked for a ruling under it and did not find one, and went back to the trough.
    64	
    65	Havel stood a moment with the key in his hand and looked at his own pins, and the street went by: a carter, and two women with a basket between them, and on the far side a man in a travelling coat with a walking stick, who glanced at the board as he glanced at everything and wrote nothing down.
    66	
    67	He had thought, when he was younger, that if it were ever said aloud something would happen.
    68	
    69	He went back in to the records table and entered the posting against the hour.
    70	
    71	The senior evaluator was still in the long room, which he had not expected. She sat in the middle chair below the covered platform with her file shut on her knee, quite alone, looking at nothing, like any woman at the end of a day's work. As he came down the side of the benches her right hand went to the breast of her coat and rested there flat for a moment, over the cloth, and came away.
    72	
    73	He knew what was under it. He had seen her fold it twice, on a desk at Norhold, a sheet that was not office stock, and put it where she had never carried anything that was the Compact's; and he had gone back to an inn that night and written his fifth. There were two of them in one town again, each with paper the office had not asked for. He had thought so once before and had taken a kind of cold comfort in it.
    74	
    75	But it was not the same now, and he made himself see why. She had not taken out the sheet in her coat, which was hers and would stay hers. She had taken out the returns, which were the registry's, and stood up in the registry's own room, and read them into the registry's own minute at a pace a clerk could follow. She had found the one door her clearance left open and had gone through it in the plainest way there was.
    76	
    77	He had kept sheets. He did not think less of them for that, since a man did what his chair allowed. But he sat down at the records table and squared the receipt book, and he knew, as he knew when a count had come out, that they were finished.
    78	
    79	---
    80	
    81	At the inn by the bridge he ate his supper downstairs among the carters and went up while it was still light.
    82	
    83	The river ran low and plain under the middle pier. He set the kit on the table by the window and unbuckled it, and took the six sheets from between the second manual and the third, and laid them out in their order, left to right, as he had laid them on the night of the seventh sitting. A mark with no office. Three between, each in its year. A sentence that shut a door. A seal on no register.
