   204	"I don't strap those," said the healer, and went after her other patient.
   205	
   206	The town went first. It went up the tiers and out by the yard gate in a slow loud river, wool-factors and carters and clerks, and he heard his name in it, the registry's and the hill's, and a good many guesses, and let them go by. The lake house's Blade came down to the rim before he left and stood looking at the oak for some time with his lath across his shoulders; then he looked at Cael, and laughed, and shook his head, and went away.
   207	
   208	The house went more slowly, for it did not seem to know how to leave. First-years stood about in knots on the tiers, re-fighting the fourth exchange with their hands. Nobody came down the steps to him, and that was Lira's doing, who had put herself at the head of the east flight with her arms folded.
   209	
   210	Withrow was the last.
   211	
   212	She had not left the registrar's chair. She sat in it, upright, with her hands in her lap, while the stand emptied round her and Bracken went down with the house's book under his arm, and she went on sitting until the tiers were bare stone again and the porter had begun at the top with his broom. Then she stood.
   213	
   214	She did not come to the steps. She looked at the oak, the whole length of it, as a woman looks at a room she has kept for a long time, and up at the first board, where four lines stood in a coach's capitals. Then she looked at Cael on his step, with his arm bound.
   215	
   216	Her face was not the registry face, and he did not know what it was. He thought that she had seen a thing on her own oak that explained two years of an enrollee looking ordinary, and that she was adding it, quite calmly, to the things she had decided not to ask about.
   217	
   218	She inclined her head to him. It was not the registry's depth; it was a good deal less, and it was meant.
   219	
   220	Then she went out by the north arch, alone, and she was the last out of the yard.
   221	
   222	On the tier above him the Current girl had come back for a scarf. She stopped beside Lira, red-eyed and hoarse.
   223	
   224	"I've trained on this hill with him for two years," she said. "I've watched every wing-three card there was. I never saw one bit of that. Not one." She wiped her nose on her wrist. "Where's he been *keeping* it?"
   225	
   226	Lira did not answer the question. She looked down the steps at him, and then out over the empty oak.
   227	
   228	"That's the first time this school has ever seen him," she said.
   229	
   230	---
   231	
   232	Ephram came back when the light had begun to go yellow.
   233	
   234	He came down the steps slowly, with his right arm bound across his chest in a sling of the healer's linen and his coat hung over the shoulder. Rooke's clerk was waiting by the boards with the chalk and the bout sheet, and Cael got up off his step and went along the stand to them.
   235	
   236	It was the yard's custom, and he had watched it from a tier a hundred times and never stood in it: after a bout on the oak the clerk chalked the result, and the two who had fought stood at the board while he did. The first board was high. The clerk set the porter's ladder against it, and Cael steadied one side of it with his one good hand and Ephram the other with his, and neither of them said that it wanted steadying.
   237	
   238	The clerk chalked the result under the notice in the same three-line grammar the yard used for every bout. Cael and Ephram stood together while he did it. Ephram looked at the board rather than at Cael, which was the register the ritual ran in.
   239	
   240	"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
   241	
   242	Cael took the offered hand with his right, which was the only one he could close, and for a moment neither of them let go. The clerk came down the ladder beside them and went away with his sheet.
   243	
   244	It was in him, new, housed for the moment where nothing else was, a thing with an edge and a rule to it. It had come out of the man in front of him an hour ago, and he could have said so in a dozen words. *There's something I took. You should know what you gave.*
   245	
   246	Ephram would have listened. He would have gone still, and thought about it, and very probably said that he was glad of that as well. But he had just said, of his own accord, that he did not need to know; he had turned his back on a table of papers twice, for a friend's sake, on the same understanding.
   247	
   248	And then he would have known. He would have been a cohort captain at a chartered house who knew what the registry's unclassified enrollee was, and what he had carried east, and he would have known it on the day a district officer rode up to the gate, and for every day after. He was staying. He had a board with his name on it, and they would come and ask him, courteously, what he had seen.
   249	
   250	Cael did not say it.
   251	
   252	"You've a look on you," said Ephram.
   253	
   254	"I'm writing something down."
   255	
   256	"You always are." Ephram took his hand back and hitched the coat on his shoulder with it. "Don't make me sound clever in it. I walked into a post."
   257	
   258	He went up the steps, slowly, and at the top he began to whistle. It was the song from the residence windows, and the third line was wrong.
   259	
   260	---
   261	
   262	He wrote it at the rim, sitting on the coping with his feet over the oak. He wrote in pencil, as Karis had learned to, with the book on his knee and the bound arm steadying the page.
   263	
   264	*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest, and the notice said "non-hostile," which it has never said, because there has never been a fight like it in the log. Ephram asked for the true measurement. He got it. He doesn't know he gave me a piece of himself for it; he consented to a fight, not to the thing the fight fed. I wrote the price down before the bout, the way the covenant says, so that when it cost — and it cost — I'd have written the cost before I collected it, which is the only line between me and what the office did at a road cut and in a records hall — spending people whose names it never bothered to write down, and asking nobody anything.*
   265	
   266	*He said "non-hostile" and the system agreed. Nine fragments and the ninth one's engagement field says the closest thing to friendship a machine knows how to file. I'll take it. It's the truest thing the instrument ever wrote about me except "no number goes here," and it was written about a fight I didn't want to win.*
   267	
   268	He read it over once. *And it cost* was true of more than he had priced the night before, and he set that down next, and then the bills, smaller.
   269	
   270	*Not priced: his shoulder. I knew a Blade can't take a commitment back. I never asked where it goes if somebody stands in the way. That's mine. He says he'd have minded more if I'd known. I mind that I didn't. Entered.*
   271	
   272	*Left forearm, wrist to elbow: the touch, and four more on the guard, the last to the bone or near it. Hip, crest to knee: ten on the oak, four past the six. Shoulder: six pushes at load, one past Norhold. Two lanes, ten and ten. Behind the eyes. One spark. Not the quiet thing; in a yard with twelve hundred people looking down it had nothing to offer and didn't. Not the one from the road.*
   273	
   274	He stopped at that, and looked at it, because it wanted its reason beside it, and the log had always been more honest than he was aloud.
   275	
   276	It had lifted its head a dozen times in four exchanges. A heel going down on oak was the plainest thing to fix in all the world, and Ephram had put his heel down thirty times; one point under that boot, set and told to stay, would have ended the bout in the first exchange. *There?* it had asked, each time, in its courteous way. *His*, he had told it. *Leave it.*
   277	
   278	*Why not. One: it's on no paper the registry holds, and a man with a pencil had the top of the west tier. Two: it came off somebody who used it to put people in a wagon, and I've not yet found a use for it that isn't a hand on a person. Three: he asked me for a fight, and it holds people. You don't do that to a friend who has asked you for a fight. It stays in the kit.*
   279	
   280	He shut the book and put it in the satchel, which Brom had left for him on the step with the strap done up.
   281	
   282	He had gone out by the yard gate after Ephram left, to put his head under the pump in the covered walk. It was a plain stone gate with a dressed lintel, and he had walked beneath it a thousand times, and halfway through, something had raised its hand.
   283	
   284	It was not the voice he knew. *There?* was a patient voice, and asked about resting things. This was quicker, and it did not ask about the lintel. It asked about the lintel's lower edge, the long straight arris of it over his head, where two faces of the stone met, and it held that edge up to him as a boy in a schoolroom holds up his slate.
   285	
   286	*Edge?*
   287	
   288	He had stood under the gate with a bound arm and very nearly laughed. It had taken him two days and forty proposals an hour to learn what to do with the last one, and he had a stair for this one to come up by.
   289	
   290	*Lintel*, he had told it. *It keeps the arch up. It isn't for cutting. Leave it.*
   291	
   292	It left it. He had felt it settle, in the place beside the other, as a second tool settles into a rack that somebody has already made room in. He supposed it would ask again. He supposed it would ask about every sill and step and table's edge between here and wherever they were going, for a day or two, and take names instead.
   293	
   294	He sat on at the rim.
   295	
   296	The sun had gone behind the lecture range. Below him the oak lay in its pit, pale and dry and empty, sixty feet by forty, with a few scuffs on it near the wall where a man had knelt. The porter had finished the tiers and gone, and up on the wall the four boards were going grey. He could still read Rooke's notice on the first, and beneath it the clerk's three chalked lines of the bout's result.
   297	
   298	He had come back from the pump by the covered walk, past the records hall.
   299	
   300	Its door had stood open, and the lamps were lit in pairs. Bracken was at his counter in his shirtsleeves, with all three pairs of glasses about him and a quire of printed forms at his elbow, on the registry's own stock, and Cael had been able to read their heading from the walk: *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Bracken had counted four sheets off the quire and laid them in a row on the wood, under the lamp. He had squared each of them to the counter's edge with one finger, and then to its neighbour, and set the pen beside them.
   301	
   302	Four. He had not looked up. They were for morning.
   303	
   304	Seln had looked round a reading room at the top of a library once, on the evening of a birthday, with six people in it and a honey cake. *It is a good room*, he had said. *I have been in a great many.*
   305	
   306	Cael had been in a great many himself by now. He had been in a wool exchange with a lot number under his boot, and a dairy with a slit window, and a borrowed office with nothing of anybody's in it; he had stood on a north road outside a room he would never go into; and he had been careful in every one of them.
   307	
   308	He looked down at sixty feet of sprung oak in a hole in the ground, where for an hour that afternoon he had not been careful with anything.
   309	
   310	It was a yard, and had no roof. He said it anyway.
   311	
   312	"Good room."

