    37	The ledger of the year went into Hesk's satchel by firelight, complete, because every year's had gone in complete and this was the ninth entry and the arc's number.
    38	
    39	The volume itself had been a birthday gift in a midwinter two years back, and had held the log through a tournament, a proceedings, and a resignation, and had about a third of its leaves left. He had carried it in Hesk's satchel since Ardenmere. Both the satchel and the volume were, as of six days ago, among the very few objects in his possession whose ownership no registry anywhere recorded, and he noticed that on the way to opening it and decided it was the least important thing he had noticed all week and wrote it down anyway.
    40	
    41	He did it in the established forms, which mattered more out here and not less. The volume out; the fire at his back for light and his knee for a desk; the margin ruled with the straight edge of Lira's route sheet for want of a rule; the head of the page dated. He set the pen down for a moment before using it, which he did once a year and had still never written about.
    42	
    43	Then the inventory, in the order they had come and not in the order they mattered, because importance was a judgment and the log did not keep judgments in the inventory column:
