    80	
    81	The letters were written after, and held for the next westbound courier whenever one existed east of the Line, which might be never, and the might-be-never was itself a fact about where they now lived.
    82	
    83	To Hesk, four pages of the tournament's own straightness — the ruling, the council, the counter, the boxes, the bout, the ford — because his grandfather read strain gauges and not polish and would want the whole run of it including the parts that did not flatter anybody. And at the end of it, the paragraph that would go in the pouch even if the four pages did not:
    84	
    85	*"Eighteen, unenrolled, and past the Line. The shape you said to mind — I minded it, all the way to the edge of the map. The tools fit the road. Mind your own shape; two polite men know your shop, and I'm not there to count them anymore. — C."*
    86	
    87	He wrote that last sentence three times before he kept it, because the first two versions were warnings dressed as news and the man deserved news dressed as news, and because Hesk had spent a lifetime declining to be protected by people who loved him and would decline it again by return of post, in about eleven words, and would be right.
    88	
    89	And to Vell, because her ledger held his first honest record and the resignation belonged in it: the season told straight and the ending told straighter, the four files closed complete and the fifth man who filed nothing, and the request that she enter it in the book at her own discretion and in her own hand — filed, he had no doubt, cross-referenced, beside a circuit rating from a smaller and more honest book, so that the one true ledger of his life ran unbroken from a border-town circuit to the edge of the administered world.
    90	
    91	It took him most of the fire's good hour, and Karis lent him the flat of a box lid to write on without being asked and took it back without comment when he was done, and Lira, passing behind him at some point, read exactly four words over his shoulder and moved on, which was as much intrusion as anyone in that family had ever permitted themselves.
    92	
    93	He addressed neither letter to anywhere he could be answered at, because there was nowhere. That was the strangest sentence of the evening and he made himself write it out plainly in the log so as not to be soft about it: *both letters go west with no return address, because I don't have one. Everyone who has ever written to me has written to a room in an institution. There isn't a room. There's a shelf above a valley, and tomorrow there'll be a different one.*
    94	
    95	He did not write to Withrow, and thought about why, and decided it was because she had said the last thing and would be irritated to have it answered; and he did not write to Ephram, because Ephram had said his last thing at a board with a towel over his shoulder and would consider a letter a demotion.
    96	
    97	---
    98	
    99	The closing window: a registry office, altitude unstated, faceless by design.
   100	
   101	The file that had been growing since a Kindling four years ago, updated by a hand no one ever attaches to a person — a clerk of some grade, in some room, working through the afternoon's amendments in the order the tray gave them, who had never seen the subject of this file and never would, and who processed it between an academy charter renewal and a lapsed sponsorship without altering the rhythm of the work.
   102	
   103	The prior designations were retired one by one, because the machine kept true records about its own lies: *enrollment: closed by resignation.* *Proceedings: moot.* *Re-evaluation: not executed.* *Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.* Four lines, each one struck through neatly and re-entered with its closing status, each one the end of an instrument that had cost somebody a year of their life to build or to survive.
   104	
   105	And the new line entered beneath them, the last institutional sentence written about him inside the world that wrote such sentences, given under the Compact's hand — both meanings, the formula and the fist, the seal on the instrument and the reach that files no paperwork — because the whole of it had been the argument that the two were one hand and had been for two hundred years:
   106	
   107	*Active non-compliance. Observation priority escalated.*
   108	
   109	The amendment took perhaps ninety seconds. The clerk blotted it, entered the date, and set the folder in the tray on the left, which in that office meant complete, and reached for the next one.
   110	
   111	Nobody in the building had ever met him. Nobody in the building had decided anything about him. Every person who had touched the file across four years had been doing their job correctly, at their grade, in order, on the record — and the file had arrived, by the accumulated work of all that correctness, at a sentence that said the registry no longer had a procedure for him and would therefore watch him instead.
   112	
   113	The window holds four seconds. It closes.
   114	
   115	The machine does not stop. It re-files. Not a machine defeated, not a secret detonated, not a system brought down — a file, updated, by nobody, same as always, and a hand that has closed on air and written down that it is waiting.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	He closed the volume and put it in the satchel, and put the satchel where he always put it, and did not put anything away after that because there was nothing else that needed putting away, and the not-having-anything-else was the last new fact of the day.
   120	
   121	The fire burned down to the good stage, the one where nobody adds anything and everybody stays anyway, and the valley below the shelf went from blue to black in about a quarter of an hour, and the first of the stars came out over a country with no name and no measurements in it.
   122	
   123	He had no tier. That was, out here, an absolutely meaningless fact about him, and he turned it over at the fire's edge for a while and found that it had changed its shape one final time. At fourteen it had been a wound; at fifteen, a problem to be managed; last winter, the whole architecture of a legal defense and the thing a continent argued about for a term. Tonight it was a piece of information with nowhere to be filed and nobody to file it, in a valley where no instrument had ever asked anybody anything, and it sat in him like a stone in a pocket that had stopped being heavy at some point in the walking without his noticing when.
   124	
   125	Karis worked. Brom checked the lashings a second time and did not pretend he was doing anything else. Lira fed the fire as though it were an opponent with a tempo, which was how she met most things. Seln sat where he could see the two hundred yards of open approach and said nothing for an hour, which from him was contentment.
   126	
   127	Cael catalogued them, because that was what he did at the end of anything that mattered, and because he had learned in five months that the only records that survive a machine are the ones kept by hand. Four people who had signed away, at a counter, in one morning, a ranking, a ladder, a certification and a career. One man who had signed away nothing because he had never signed anything, and who had left a set of keys in a row on a blotter instead. A cart. Three boxes and a case under one canvas. A horse. A valley with no name in it.
   128	
   129	It was, by every instrument the continent owned, less than any of them had possessed a week ago. He had run that arithmetic four separate times on the road east and it came out the same way every time, and he had stopped running it, because there was a column the instruments did not have and every single one of the assets was in it.
   130	
   131	"I have nine things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no enrollment, no protection — and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them, and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me, and one taken in earnest from a friend, and the machine's own maintenance records in a case under that canvas, which we can't file and can't publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can't be spent."
   132	
   133	He turned it over once and let it stand.
   134	
   135	"And somewhere behind us, a file with a new sentence in it, written by nobody, same as always."
   136	
   137	Lira, feeding the fire: "Let them escalate. They'll need better maps."
   138	
   139	Brom: "And better roads."
   140	
   141	Karis, writing — the field-archive open on her knee, the first entry of a new record in a place that had none: "Noted."
   142	
   143	Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the dark east horizon with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home.
   144	
   145	The hand had signed its last instrument with his name on it, and the name was still wrong, and the wrongness was still the only true thing in the file. He wasn't inside its jurisdiction anymore. The fire burned in a territory no Arbiter had ever measured, above a valley with no name, on a road that ran the right direction — and for the first time in four years, nothing anywhere was deciding what he was, except him.

