     1	# Chapter 24 — Nine
     2	
     3	The first true edge-territories camp was a switchback shelf above an unnamed valley, and Cael counted it in the way he had counted in every room of his life, because the habit had outlived its reason and he had decided to keep it.
     4	
     5	They had come down off the ridge at the sixth day's noon and found the track turning back on itself four times across a slope of loose grey scree, and on the third turn the shelf: forty feet by fifteen of level ground, dry, with the rock face at its back and a lip of thorn and rowan at its edge, a spring coming out of the face at the north end and running off the shelf's lip in a thread. The valley lay below it and had no name. It had a river in it, and a floor of birch scrub going gold, and on the far side a scree slope with a track cut across it by something that was not people, and nothing else anywhere: no field, no wall, no smoke, no cut wood, nothing at all that said a person had ever had an intention here.
     6	
     7	Seln had walked it first, as he walked everything first, and came back and said, "One approach, and it comes up in the open for two hundred yards. Water at the north end. If it rains hard the shelf sheds east, so nothing sleeps at the east end." That was the whole of his report and it was the reason they camped there.
     8	
     9	They got in at the eighth hour and had the camp made by the tenth, and the making of it was the first work any of them had ever done that no institution had a form for.
    10	
    11	There was a discussion about the watch, and it was the evening's only argument, and it was short. Lira wanted three watches of three hours. Seln said two of four, and said why: three watches meant three men sleeping badly and one of them badly enough to be useless at noon, and a party of five with one cart and one road did not need three sets of eyes, it needed two sets that worked. Lira looked at that for a moment and said, "Fine — but not you and not Brom on the same one, because you'll both take the bad half and neither of you will admit it," which was so exactly correct that Seln declined to answer it and Brom laughed, once, quietly, into the lashings.
    12	
    13	Cael took the second watch and got, for the first time in four years, a genuinely useless one. There was nothing to watch. He sat at the shelf's lip with the fire behind him and the two hundred yards of open approach in front and ran the old instrument over a country that returned nothing: no light, no movement, no grade, no coat, no seal, no glass. Somewhere down the valley something barked twice and stopped. He caught himself, at about the second hour, charting the stars into columns out of sheer professional habit, and stopped, and laughed at himself, and then went back to doing it, because the alternative was sitting in the dark not counting anything and he had never once managed that.
    14	
    15	The found family ran its old grammar on new terrain, which was the first thing Cael noticed and the thing he wrote down first, because it was not obvious and had not been guaranteed.
    16	
    17	Brom's quiet competence, two years aimed at acquiring dinner twice over, was aimed now at load-balancing a cart for a road with no waystations. He had the whole of it off and spread on the shelf's stone before anybody else had unrolled anything: the boxes, the kits, the tools, the grain, the case; and he went through it once with his hands and once with his eyes and then rebuilt the load with the heavy weight low and forward and the water where a man could reach it walking, and he moved four things from the cart to the panniers, and he did not explain any of it. When Lira asked him what the rule was he said, "Nothing you'll want tomorrow goes under anything you won't," and went back to the lashings. Cael entered that as a sentence worth keeping and noted that Brom, who had never in four years volunteered a general principle about anything, had produced one within a day of the road having no rules on it.
    18	
    19	Karis indexed her three boxes into a working field-archive by firelight, which took her the whole of the evening and outlasted two rebuildings of the fire. The system was a good one because she had been designing it in a coach for five days: three boxes, four categories, a running index in the notebook's back matter, and every bundle re-tied with a cloth tag she inked herself. She was building the room she had lost — the annex's north room, the lock, the warrant — into an architecture that needed no lock, because it needed no institution, because a system carried on a cart cannot be revoked at a counter by a person who has never read it.
    20	
    21	"It's worse," she said, when Cael asked her how it compared. "It's slower to search and it will be damp within a month and I will lose things. It is also the first archive I have ever kept that nobody can close." She tied a tag. "I have been thinking about which of those facts is larger. I have concluded that the question is badly posed and that I should stop asking it and go on working, which is the finding I reach about most questions and is why I am tolerable to travel with."
    22	
    23	Lira mapped tomorrow's route the way she mapped a bracket sheet, flat on a stone with a stub of charcoal: the valley's exits, three of them; the ridge's approaches; the water; the two lines a party with a cart could take and the one line a party without a cart could take if it had to leave the cart. She annotated the difficult stretch with the same shorthand she used for a difficult opponent, which Cael could read and nobody else could, and when she finished she looked at the whole sheet for a while and said, "It's the same work. Nobody told me it was going to be the same work."
    24	
    25	Seln was gone for an hour after they made camp and came back with the lay of every approach for a mile, unasked, and reported it in four sentences and did not sit down until he had finished. A man who had spent fifteen years protecting people who did not know it was not going to stop now that they did; the only difference was that he now said the findings out loud, which was, in its way, the largest change any of them had undergone.
    26	
    27	The practical question got asked over the meal, by Lira, in the way Lira asked practical questions, which was without warm-up: "What do we do for money."
    28	
    29	It was a good question and it had four answers and none of them was frightening, which surprised all of them a little. Karis had the sum they had left, to the copper, and read it out, and it was more than any of them had expected because five people had spent a term being fed and housed by an institution and had not been spending. Brom said that a big man with a cart and a horse is employable anywhere there is anything to move, and said it without any of the self-deprecation the sentence invited, because it was simply true and he had done it before. Lira observed that unadministered country has fights in it and that fights in unadministered country get paid in a currency nobody has to verify, and got a look from Karis and said, "I said *observed*." And Seln, who had been silent, said, "Nine hamlets in four days, going by the keeper's account of the road, and not one of them has had a person come through who can read a contract. There is your first six months," and went back to his food, and Karis wrote it down, which meant it was decided.
    30	
    31	Nobody said the other thing, which was that four of the five of them had never in their lives been unemployed and that the fifth had been unemployed in every way that mattered for fifteen years while drawing a salary.
    32	
    33	And Ephram's absence at the fire was noted once, without grief. He had stayed. Staying was right for him, and had been right for him since the trial, and would go on being right for him; and the family's arithmetic held him anyway, the way it held everyone it had decided to keep, present or not. Lira said his name once during the evening, in a sentence about a bout two years old, and nobody made anything of it, and that was the correct handling and everybody at the fire knew it.
