editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:93:He had watched a great many people stand on their marks and wait. Dessa waited like a wall; Corbin waited like a man reading a newspaper. This woman waited like somebody who had shut every door in a house, one after another, and was now standing in the hall listening to be sure that none of them would open. She had done it well. She had done it in order, with the words in the order the Handbook gave them, and she had not reached for the book once. If he had not sat at Torvin's kitchen table until the window went grey, he would have been sitting in the hall with her now, listening to the doors stay shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-cold-prompt.md:123:Cael ate and listened and minded his edges. Across the hall a table of Mire second-years were telling each other in low voices what the Archmarshal had eaten at supper, which none of them could possibly know. At the next table a Blade fourth-year was explaining to a first-year, at length and with diagrams drawn in spilt water, exactly how an inspection worked, and Cael listened to that for a while because the fourth-year was wrong in a very confident and orderly way. Then he stopped listening, because the stopping was also practice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-cold-prompt.md:1125:He did not feel it go. That was the whole trouble with it, and he knew it, and it still caught him. One moment he was listening to a clerk explain a seal. The next, with no seam between them, the clerk was saying something else, and there was a short piece of the sitting he could not account for, as if somebody had turned a page in his head while he was not looking. He took the corner back at once. Nobody had looked his way. The inky-knuckled clerk on the next chair was writing.

