editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-009/protected-text.stdout.log:7896:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. It is the first First-day in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:Bracken wound the counter clock on the Seventh-day before the first bell, and nobody had ever been allowed to speak to him while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:They were as he had squared them at dusk. Cael had read their heading from the covered walk, with his arm new in its binding, and had thought then that four sheets of paper was a small thing for a year to come to; and he thought so still. A challenge had come up the bluff road under escort, with outriders, and been read aloud standing, and the answer to it had weighed eleven pounds four ounces. This would not have tipped the post-room's beam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:17:She read the first leaf from its head to its foot, which took her no time at all. *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Her name stood on it in Bracken's small upright hand, and under it *Wind*, and *Iron, Rank One*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:"It stays true. A house does not certify a tier and cannot uncertify one, and that line is on the form only so that the registry may know whom it is reading about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She did not leave it blank. She wrote three words on it, in the capitals she kept for the foot of a priced leaf, and turned the sheet round so that the registrar could read them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:31:Bracken read it twice, by the movement of the one pair of glasses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:59:"I shall leave it where it is." Bracken did not blot the leaf at once. He looked at the name on it, in a hand that had gone through the paper, and Cael, who was near enough to see, saw him move the leaf an eighth of an inch so that it lay true to the one beside it. "For the record, and because nobody else in this hall will tell you so: I have seen eight advancement dockets go down from this hill, and seven of the eight were signed at this counter with whatever pen came to hand. I shall remember the one that was not, and I shall remember which pen was used for what."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:63:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:67:He had come to this counter in his first week with a provision and a stamp, and been entered. He had borrowed the code from it by volume, and brought every volume back by the second bell, and he had stood here to read a deposit book upside down. In all that time Bracken had never once asked him why he wanted a thing; he had asked only when.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:81:Cael wrote the day. He had held nothing but a pencil since the rim of the Crown yard, and his left arm lay along the counter in its binding, stiff from the wrist to the elbow, and steadied the leaf by its weight alone. He wrote the name as the leaf had it, all of it, in the careful hand he kept for things other people would read, and it looked like a stranger's; he supposed that was correct too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:87:He had not blotted it. He turned the leaf a quarter round on the counter, so that its left margin lay toward him, and he looked up at the clock he had just wound, and then he dipped the pen himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:89:He wrote in the margin. It was not the upright hand of his entries but the other, the sloped longhand of his private daybook, which Cael had seen perhaps four times, and he wrote three lines of it, slowly and close, running up the edge of the form where the registry had printed nothing. Then he turned the leaf back, and Cael read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:99:Then he opened the enrollment book. It was the current volume, the tall one, in the house's own boards, and he turned to a leaf near the front and then to three others, and at each he wrote the same thing against a name. He did it four times, without hurrying, and Cael read the fourth upside down, as he had read most of what he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:105:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:"It is," said Bracken. He had a second paper ready beside her leaf, and it ran to a full sheet. "An enrollee who holds standing under the research council does not only withdraw. She lapses, by heads, and the house is bound to read her the heads, so that nobody may say afterward that a thing was taken from her unawares. I drew it from the council's minute, and I believe it agrees with a sheet of your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Bracken read. He read as he had read the fourteenth chapter to a table on the first night, flat and exact, giving each line the same weight as the last, and after each he stopped until she answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:143:It was no longer than it takes to draw a breath, and anybody else in the hall would have missed it. Cael did not, for he had heard her read that head from her own sheet two nights ago, and it had taken her half a second longer than the rest then as well. It was a small locked press on the library's second floor, with a ledger chained to it, and she had told him once that she had read her way round its shelves like a person eating a loaf from the outside in, saving the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:"Lapsed." She put her hand to her collar and drew out the cord. The key was on it, brass, worn bright at the bow by a year against her skin, and she lifted the cord over her head, and held it, and for a moment she did not put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:"There is one line more on the council's minute," he said, and he took up the sheet again. "*The enrollee's tier, as certified by the registry: Ember; Iron, Rank Three.* I have not read it to you, and I have struck it from this schedule, in ink, with my initials against the stroke, because it is not the house's. It was never the house's to grant, and it does not lapse with a room." He looked at her over the glasses. "Nobody can take a tier from you, Advocate. They can only decline to count it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:165:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:"Tomorrow, at the first bell, I shall open the daybook and enter the post. On the next Seventh-day I shall wind that clock. In the week after the close a clerk will come up the road with a stamp for the chancellor's folder, and I shall see that he is handed the right volume and initials his own line." He took the last pair of glasses off his forehead and laid all three in a row. "I tell you so because you might suppose, after this year, that a man in my chair would go about it differently. I have asked myself whether I ought. I intend to keep true records, as I have kept them, and four files will go down to the district in tomorrow's bag with not a line in them that I could not swear to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"It has not been cheap. I weighed your answer on the post-room's beam and was proud of the figure. I lit this hall by my own authority, and read you the code until morning, and told a table of tired people that it was real law and that we should read it. I should like one thing said at this counter, by me, since nobody will enter it." He cleared his throat. "I was wrong about what the reading would buy you. I have known it since a seal came to my gate that I could not place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:183:"There is one thing more," said Bracken, in his ordinary voice, and put one pair of glasses back on. "The evaluator of record was at this counter before I had finished with the clock. He does not speak to me while I wind it; he is the only officer of the house who has never needed telling. He left a leaf from his book, which is his last note upon the provision's enrollee, and he asks that it be entered with the withdrawal. He asked also that you should read it here, before I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:*Assay-provision enrollment: resigned in good standing prior to revocation date. Baseline file: closed complete. Four terms of evaluation-linked observation, no adverse finding, no anomaly of conduct. The evaluator of record notes that the file closes on the enrollee's own act and not the registry's, and that this is the correct closing, and the only just one available. The instruments will not see its like.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:189:He read it through, and then he read its last line again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:247:The rotation's book was in his coat. It was a thin grey thing, cheaper than any other book he owned, and it held a year and a half of Fifth-days: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure each was meant to buy. Its last line said *All of it.* It was a true record. It was also the only paper on the hill that could turn a house found compliant into a house that had signed figures an enrollee arranged, and Gault's name stood under every one of those figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:249:He carried it down the corridor to the stove at the stair's head, which the porter kept in through the last week, for the damp. He opened the door and fed it the book a gathering at a time, and watched each one catch and go grey and fall in. It was the first record he had ever destroyed, and he thought that Karis would have understood it, and that Seln would not have needed to be told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:9:That was not, in itself, a thing to write down, for a man could be late. But the clerk sat with his pencil lifted, and found that he could not go on to his column. In two years the teaching assistant had never once been late. He had never been early either. He had come in by the inner door at the same moment each day, without a sound, so that the clerk had mostly not noticed him arrive, and had only looked up and found the table occupied, as one finds that a clock has been wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:33:The clerk read that last sheet through, standing, and there was not an error in it. There had never been an error in any of them. He had been corrected by that hand himself, once, in a margin, in the seventh week of this year. *Both seen*, the margin had said, and he had been angry for a day and then grateful.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:37:He went to the Magister at the second bell and was told to write what he had found and nothing he supposed. He wrote *The teaching assistant not at his table. Keys left on the blotter, four. No word.* The Magister read it and wrote something in his small book, and sent him across the court to the registrar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:41:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:The registrar did what a registrar does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:It was the right question, the one any careful officer asks when a quiet man goes. Nobody at that desk thought he had taken anything. He had sat at a copying table, and held four keys, and laid all four out in a row. But for two years he had been closer to one subject than anybody else in the service, and before that he had been close to sixty-two others, and he had watched, and written what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:77:They read fifteen years of it, and every line was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:87:It knew what he had sent. It did not know what he had kept. There was no return that listed the things a quiet man had declined to write down. Nor was there any record of a records room on a top floor, in a district he had left twelve years before, where he had gone to look for a subject's travel papers. Nobody thought to ask for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:91:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He did not read them. There was nothing in them to read; he had seen to that when he wrote them. He untied each tape, and laid the leaves in by the handful, and waited until they had gone grey before he put in the next. A river town went first, where he had learned to fold a return. Then a guild hall, and a district seat in an old building, which he fed in rather quickly. There was an academy with four subjects, and a station in the hills with a green corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:111:After the tenth bell he laid out the keys. There were four, and he set them on the blotter in a row, by size, and turned the bows one way. It was not a message. It was a courtesy, to a clerk who added his column aloud and would be alarmed in the morning, since a man who finds the keys does not have to wonder what has been taken, and need only count them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:117:He did not go by the gate. The lodge-man kept a column for every bell, and Seln had read that column on a good many Seventh-days. He went by the stable passage and the farm track, as he had taken four people on a night in the recess, through an orchard that was black and smelled of wet bark. He walked down the middle of the track, where the grass was shortest, and at the gardener's gate he lifted the latch and let it down behind him without a click. It swung true; the large one had mended its top hinge with a mallet and a wedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:125:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:175:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:Forty paces. It was his own rule, and he had given it in an orchard with the reasons: water covers a voice at forty paces and a foot at ten, and things walk to water at night. He had told them it was to be forty paces of the shortest person present, and the researcher had written in her notebook, quite seriously, *of whose?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:195:He kept arriving at that as he worked. They would have come from first light with a loaded cart, and a ford, and a horse none of them knew. The boy would have an arm he could not close and two days of bills come due at once, and the girl would have talked to the horse the whole way and would be hoarse. They would come round that thorn at dusk, tired through, and cold, since people are always cold at the end of the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:He read it over. Every word was true. It was the last of its kind, and it was as good as any of them; a reader could have checked each line against a book and found it stood, and he would have learned nothing about a boy who had let a coat fall in front of twelve hundred people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:7:The seat's clerk read the margin twice. He had no heading for a margin, and he entered the leaf as received.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:He then had before him a ruling, given under the Compact's hand two days earlier, and an enrollment which that ruling revoked from the year's close; and the enrollment had ended of itself before the close arrived. He turned to the code. It had a word for a matter that has lost its subject, and he wrote the word in the proceedings' last column, small, and blotted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:There remained the order for re-evaluation, and that was not the clerk's to close. It had a date upon it, and a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:The order was called, and the name was read as the instruments had it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:The code gave him his phrase. An order was executed when its hour had been kept and its room opened, and this one had been executed, in every particular, against nobody. He wrote *Re-evaluation: not executed; evaluand not produced*, which was how the form said the same thing. The president read the line and initialled it. Then the officer put the cover back over the frame and locked the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:In the week after the close a junior clerk went up a bluff road with a stamp. He wrote in the right-hand column of a charter folder that the respondent institution had been found compliant. The chancellor gave him tea, and he said afterward that she had been perfectly civil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:57:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:67:He read the leaf from its head to its seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:89:*The house's record is the soundest I have reviewed.* That was true as well, and it would be cited against the next house, to show how little a sound record weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:95:He did not sign it. He did not write *seen*, or *no observation*, both of which were forms, and each of which would have meant that the office had looked and was content. He turned the leaf face down in the tray marked *done*, exactly as it had come to him, with the margin white from its head to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:109:He drew the next thing toward him. It was a hill station's request for a second frame, and he read it through from its head to its seal, and dipped his pen, and answered it in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:151:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:175:There would be a line like it about them at every water from here to the last stamp. *Four, a cart, eastbound.* He found that he did not mind. It was true, and whoever read it would learn only that they had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:189:"Nothing," said Cael. "He's doing his work. He's to follow until somebody tells him to stop, and nobody can get word to him out here. He'll stop when his road does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:203:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:245:"Of yours," said Seln. "You are the shortest person present. I shortened my stride, and it took me three attempts to get it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:279:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:283:The second day was longer than the first, and wetter underfoot, and the stones went by. *Twenty-four. Twenty-five.* The road was still metalled, and still ditched. At a place where another road came in from the south, there was an iron post with a hook on it and a leather bag hung on the hook, sealed with the registry's lead. A rider would come for it, and leave an empty one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:289:"Yes. That as well." Karis walked on. "I shall miss being queried. It meant somebody had read me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:301:"A fortnight. She said a fortnight." He turned the forearm in its strap. The plum had not faded, but the five bars across it had begun to blur at their edges, and he could close the hand halfway. "It's the only one I'll still be carrying past the last stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:She wrote quickly and without looking up. The instructor went on, about a fighter who chose her foot late and a table that would read the choosing as a fault, and how many exchanges it took a good panel to learn otherwise, and Lira turned a leaf. She asked one question, which he could not hear, and was answered at some length, and wrote the answer down as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:37:"Leave the door open as you go, would you," said the instructor. "It's close in here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:41:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:63:He had not answered. He had walked past her to his table, where the red book lay, and opened it, and he did not have to hunt for the place. He turned the book round on the table and went to the window with his back to her, and stood there looking at the court, so that she might read it or leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:65:She had gone and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:93:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:107:"Then the wing receives it." He put out a hand, and took the card, and read both sides, and laid it in a tray. "The provision's hours were complete on the Fourth-day of the week before last. The card agrees. It is pleasant when they agree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:115:Cael read it once, and that was enough; he had kept longer things than that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:157:"It's from my mother," he said. "Or my grandmother, in my mother's hand. It's got the ledger in it, however they've put it this time. They're neither of them unkind. They'd have the finding by now, from the provincial counter, and they'll be glad of it, and they'll be right to be." He looked at the red seal across the room. "If I open that, I've read it, and then I'm a man who read what his family asked and went east anyway. I'd rather be a man who didn't know the particulars. It's the same road, and it's lighter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:167:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:255:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:265:She had come down from her own range on her own feet. He had known her come to that gate once before, for four riders, when she had stood where she stood now, in the twelve feet of shadow under the gatehouse, and had an instrument read aloud. She had nothing in her hands today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:269:He had thought about what she might say. She had forbidden him to thank her, and she did not make farewells, and she had told him in her office that the record would say he left, and what she meant to do about that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:291:Rooke looked at Lira, and she looked back, and neither of them said anything at all, since it had been said at dawn, over a book. Then he looked at Brom, who was at the tailboard with the last of the rope in his hands. Brom had stood in a district's frame two days ago and felt a plate come up warm, and had left the paper for it under a film of dust; and Rooke knew that, for he had heard everything on the hill by the second bell of any morning, and had once had a meet record kept under a hall's seal for a fighter because paper could not be rubbed off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:305:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:321:Cael could have given him the line, having read enough of the kind on a table at the top of the library. *Subject left the house at dusk, on foot, with three others and a cart. Status changed.* It would be true, every word, and it would go west by the morning bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:59:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:115:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:"This is the first archive I have ever kept that nobody can close," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:Karis read out the purse after that, since she had kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:177:She read the total. Not one of them had guessed so high.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:She read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:189:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:207:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:237:It was his own hand at fifteen, rounder than his hand was now, and pressing harder. It was a boy's writing, and the boy had been frightened and was setting things down in order so as not to be. He read the entry through. He knew it by heart, and he read it anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:245:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:259:*I can work within the system. I have the receipts: a hearing, a provision, a stamp renewed four times, a tournament their own clause put me in. The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed. This is more text. We'll read it better than they do. We always have.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:261:He read it through. He had expected to wince, and he did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:263:It had been true. That was the thing he had not been able to see for most of a year, when he had thought the second half of the year had proved the first half wrong. But they *had* read it better. Karis had read it better than a procurator and a hooded seat together, for fifty-one minutes, and nobody had contradicted her. The text had held, to the last leaf. He had left the house by a door in the code, and it had been entered in a registrar's hand in five words; he had worked within the system all the way to the end of the system, and walked off the edge of it in good standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:271:He read the two of them together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:281:The one to Vell was shorter. She had kept a ledger that rated him before any registry did, and he had written to her at the close of each year. He told her that the year had closed, and that he had been found lawful and revoked in one paragraph. He wrote that he did not think her ledger had ever been wrong about him, and that he was now somewhere it could not be checked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:287:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:323:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:325:"They will not close it," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:339:He had written it all down. It was on the leaf, in order, with one line in it that had no name. He had been keeping that kind of list since he was fourteen, and he had always kept it where nobody could read it over his shoulder. He looked round the fire, at four faces lit from below, and counted them, from habit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:361:The fire burned. Below the shelf a valley lay in the dark, with water going through it, and nobody had ever told him what it was called; beyond it there were seven ridges that he had counted, and more that he had not. Brom went back to the lip and sat down facing outward. After a little while Seln lay down under the pine, on his side, with the satchel under his head, and shut his eyes as a man does who has decided to trust a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:365:He had been read at fourteen, in a room, in eleven seconds, and since that morning there had always been somebody deciding what he was. A warden had done it, and a provost, and a tournament office; a seat had done it under a hood, and a designation had done it that no office would own. Some of them had been kind, and most had been correct, and all of them had been writing in a column that he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He did not put it in the book. It was only a column in his head, and each day it was shorter than the day before. On the first day it had run to a dozen things: metalled stone underfoot, ditches, dressed milestones with the district's device, a keeper at the ford with a stamp, a chartered sign, a board of hours. On the second day there had been a post bag on a hook, sealed in lead, and a notice of tolls in the registry's print, and a rider in district grey going west at a trot, who had not looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:They came to the top in the last of the light. The land lay over on its back there, wide and bare and rolling, with the wind going across it, and half an hour on stood a low building with a lamp in its window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:The board hung from an iron arm over the door, with the registry's seal burned into it, and the wind had swung it against the wall so often that it had worn a pale groove in the stone. There was a yard with a wall round it, and a well with a lid, and a stack of cut turf against the gable, higher than a man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He was old, and bent a little to one side, as a thorn bends that has had the wind on it all its life. He held the lantern up and looked at the horse first, and then at the cart. He walked round to the tailboard and lifted the canvas's edge with two fingers, as a man looks under a cloth at a market, and let it drop again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:75:Cael stopped in front of it, and Karis was there before him. It was the registry's own print, under glass, with the seal at its head, and it was the last such board on that road. They had passed one at every waystation since Ostrand, and he had read each of them in passing, as he read everything; he read this one through, standing, with his arm aching in its strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:"So it says." The keeper had hung his lantern on its nail. "I've never seen one come back in under thirty. It goes down that hill in a bag, when there's a bag, and somebody looks at it, and it comes up again. A man could walk to the district and back in the time, and some have." He lowered himself onto the bench by the fire. "Mostly they don't wait. They read that, and they stand where you're standing, and work it out, and then they go on east without, or they go home."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:97:Brom came and stood at Cael's shoulder. He read the notice down, from *Copper* to *Gold*, and he was a long while at the second word. Then he read the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:103:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:It was the last day of the academic year. Cael had known it when he woke, as one knows a date that has been set against one's name for a long time. Somewhere behind him, six switchbacks up from a river, a bell would ring that evening and the house would close its books; and at some hour between, on a north road short of a toll-bar, a stout fair man would unlock a windowless building. He did not know the hour, for Withrow's sheet had given only the day, and he found that he kept looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:The road went on across the upland. There was no stone of any kind now, only the track, two ruts and a ridge of grass, and the wind, and now and then a sheep that lifted its head. A little before noon they came to a pillar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:157:There had been a ferry there once. Cael saw the posts first, two on each bank, black and leaning, and a flat boat lying on the near shingle with its bottom stove in and grass growing through it. It had been kept for when the water was up and a loaded wagon could not ford. A board lay face down in the grass by the nearer post, and he turned it over with his foot. The paint had gone, but the letters had been cut before they were painted, and he could read them. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* The registry's seal was at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:193:He knew that narrow column. All five of them knew it, for they had sat round a table at the top of a library and read forty of its lines aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:219:"The wall's built round it." Karis had come close, and was looking at the joint. "The courses are cut to fit; you can see where the mason trimmed them. So the stone was here first, and they brought the house to it." She turned to the counter, through the open door. "The register has a first leaf. It says the station was established *at the sigil*. I took that for a manner of speaking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:Nobody added to that, for there was nothing to add that would not have been a guess. The wind went over the roof, and the dark block sat in its notch with its mark on it, as it had sat before anybody built a wall there to keep the rain off a clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:233:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:277:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:325:It was very quiet, for the wind had dropped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:349:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:371:It was soft at the folds by now. It had come up a bluff in a post-room sort in the year's seventh week, and he had read it some dozens of times, at a sill, and knew it without the flame. A Gold at Auremont had written to tell him not to mistake what was happening to him for procedure. She had said that the rematch stood, and she had told him to keep himself unclassified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:373:He had not answered, for there had been no answer he could put into a pouch that a clerk would read for completeness. There was no pouch out here. He tore a clean leaf from the back of the observation notebook and laid it on the book's cover, under the pin of light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:381:He read it over once. It was true, and there was nothing in it that he would have minded a clerk copying. He folded it in three and wrote her name on the outside, and *Auremont*, and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:401:"That's a post-rider," said Seln. "Not the registry's. There is no lead on the bags."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:421:"That's what I'm for." The man took it and read the outside, slowly, with his lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:439:He sat down on the bank with Hesk's book on his knee. It was his log, where he set down what he carried and what he owed. One promise had gone west that minute in a mule's bag, and it was answered, not paid. The other was older, and nobody had been written to about it at all. He put the two side by side on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:445:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:459:They stood on the ridge in the wind, the five of them, and looked at an old brown horse. He had been bought for a hill a day, and he had done one each day, honestly, for five days, and this was a second in one morning, after a river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:479:He did not see it coming. The track went up between two rocks, and then there was no more up, and the wind hit him in the face. He walked forward three paces to get out of the cart's way, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:485:There was a valley below, deep and long, with a thread of water in it. Beyond the valley was another ridge, higher than this, dark with trees he could not name from here, and beyond that another, paler, and another after that, the colour of smoke; he counted seven before the air took them. There was no road on any of it but the two ruts at his feet. He saw no smoke, and no roof. The wind came over it from somewhere that nobody had drawn, and it smelled of nothing he knew.

