editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:27:- **The Registry Line**: what the road still had, day by day; the last chartered waystation and its keeper ("You've boxes."); the last tier notice with a hay sum; **the last watcher's nod to Brom**; **the last station** (swept; roof mended by someone; the book that stops four years ago with its open line); five crossings, each in a sentence; **"You couldn't read me… Thanks for the eleven seconds."**; the Line, unmarked; **"Nobody's watching."**; the first night outside any jurisdiction; **the reply to Daeva** sent west with the post-rider at the ford; "Both promises ride east with me."; the first ridge — **"Nobody mentions that it's big."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:"You've boxes," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:67:"Carters have bales," said the keeper. "I've had carters forty years. Bales and sacks, and a sheet I put my mark on. Folk who are running have bundles, and I get three or four of those a year: a blanket with the corners tied, and they won't meet my eye, and they're gone before I've the fire up." He scratched his jaw with the lantern's ring. "I don't get boxes. Roped, with a knot like that. Somebody took time over those, and you don't take time if you're running."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:353:It came out of the west, behind them, and it stayed. By the middle of the afternoon the track was running, and by dusk they could not see a hundred yards. There was no thorn up there, and no wall. Brom chocked the cart on the lee side of a low bank and they ran the canvas out from its bed on two of the gelding's hobble-stakes, and all five of them got in under it, with the boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:361:It was the first night of his life outside any jurisdiction, and he lay under a cart and waited to see what that was like. It was wet, and his arm ached. Lira, on watch, was telling the gelding that he had been perfectly right about the hill. Karis had her back against a box and her knees drawn up. Nobody on the continent was required to write a line about any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:465:"All three. It's the weight on his back end, going up. We take them up by hand, and the cart goes up light, and they go on again at the top."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:487:Nobody spoke. Brom had put the first box down and was standing with his hands open, and Karis had let go of the horse.

